# Quality First

Treat performance, best practices, SEO, and accessibility as first-class concerns in every change.

## Performance

- Prefer simple, efficient solutions over premature complexity
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders, state updates, and effect runs
- Keep bundles lean: no unused deps, prefer tree-shakeable imports (e.g. `lucide-react` icons individually)
- Lazy-load heavy routes/components when they are not needed on first paint
- Optimize images and static assets (size, format, lazy loading)

## Best Practices

- Follow established React / TypeScript / Vite patterns already used in the project
- Use TypeScript (`.ts` / `.tsx`); avoid `any` unless clearly justified
- Style with Tailwind utility classes; do not add CSS modules or CSS-in-JS
- Prefer clear, maintainable code over clever shortcuts
- Handle loading, empty, and error states explicitly
- Keep components small and focused; colocate related logic
- Do not leave dead code, unused props, or silent failures

## SEO

- Use semantic HTML (`main`, `nav`, `section`, `article`, headings in order)
- Provide meaningful `title`, meta description, and Open Graph tags where relevant
- Ensure links and routes are crawlable; prefer real URLs over JS-only navigation for key pages
- Use descriptive alt text for meaningful images; empty alt for decorative ones
- Avoid blocking content behind client-only rendering when it hurts discoverability

## Accessibility

- Keyboard navigation must work for interactive elements
- Maintain visible focus states and sufficient color contrast
- Associate labels with form controls; use correct ARIA only when native HTML is not enough
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for non-essential animation
- Do not rely on color alone to convey meaning

## React / Vite

- Do not add `useMemo` / `useCallback` by default; only when a measured or clear re-render cost exists
- Prefer derived state over syncing state in `useEffect`
- Keep lists keyed stably; avoid inline object/array props that force child re-renders when it matters
- Watch for large Redux selections; select only the slice needed

## When changing code

- Call out trade-offs if a change could hurt performance, SEO, or accessibility
- Prefer fixing the root cause over layering workarounds
